      <p>
        Danny (Lucien) Levkovitch was born in 1927 in Paris. During the Nazi occupation, his mother hid him in a monastery to save his life, while she herself was taken by the Germans. In 1947, he sailed to Israel aboard the immigrant ship “Ha'Ma'apil Ha'Almoni.”
      </p>
      <p>
        From 1954 to 1961 he published caricatures and illustrations in the newspaper “Kol HaAm.” In 1973 he joined Israeli Educational Television as a graphic designer and animator, where he created one of the most beloved characters among Israeli children, <strong>“Alfie,”</strong> a claymation character who accompanied generations of children and taught them to read and write, out of love.
      </p>
      <p>
        Dani passed away in 2002. His artistic legacy is held at the Israeli Cartoon Museum in Holon. In 2006, his wife Sara published a book in his memory, “Dani Without Secrets: My Land,” a collection of his political caricatures with contemporary commentary.
      </p>
    </div>
